  "query_preview": "..\nA weak shortwave trough is expected to bring a front across the \nregion Wednesday morning through Thursday morning, causing rain \nshowers to move from the northwest to the southeast. This initial \nsystem is expected to be relatively light, with higher confidence \nin 24-hour precipitation totals along the coast ranging from 0.25 \nto 0.5 inches. Amplification by orographic lifting could cause \nwindward areas to see totals on the higher end of this range. \nPrecipitation will taper off Thursday night into Friday morning as\nhigh pressure briefly builds aloft and helps to clear skies.\n\nConditions are expected to deteriorate again Friday night, as a \nstronger front approaches from a low moving into the north-central\ngulf. This system will bring more significant precipitation to \nthe gulf coast, spreading across the entire panhandle through the \nweekend. While guidance on the low's progression varies, high \nconfidence exists for 24-hour totals of 0.5 to 0.75 inches, with \npotential for 1.25 to 1.75 inches in some models. The heaviest \nimpacts are anticipated along the coast from Yakutat to Sitka, \nwith significant orographic enhancement expected in the \nFairweather, Chilkat, and Coast Ranges, likely increasing totals \nin inland areas such as Juneau, Petersburg, and Ketchikan. \nAdditionally, as the front pushes inland Saturday, the increased \npressure gradient will strengthen winds, specifically in Lynn \nCanal and between Cape Suckling and Cape Fairweather, where a weak\nbarrier jet may form as the low moves further east.\n\nTemperatures through this period will persist in the upper 50s \nand 60s, with potential to see upper 60s further inland on Friday,\ngiven ample clearing.",
